Apple's latest based Mac books are of  really great value compared to last year  especially the base MacBook Pro which  now packs a powerful quad-core processor  the touch bar and the t2 chip and what's  most impressive is that it's still  priced at only $1300 and it performs  close to as well as the more expensive  eighteen hundred dollar model the  MacBook Air didn't really change much at  all since last year but it actually got  $100 cheaper so you may be asking what's  the catch how was Apple able to offer so  much more value this year and did they  have to sacrifice something the answer  is yes they did and it has to do with  the SSD storage after some testing with  the new 2019 MacBooks we found that the  storage transfer speeds have actually  gone down this year for example the 2017  $1300 MacBook Pro with the base 128  gigabyte SSD got around 2300 megabytes  per second read speed compared to only  around 1,300 megabytes per second on the  2019 based model that's a 1,000 megabyte  per second drop in speed and even if you  upgrade to the 256 gig SSD it's still  around a thousand megabytes per second  slower than the 256 gig on the $1,800  MacBook Pro which shows that only the  base model has been affected and we see  the same thing on the MacBook Air as  well this year's model dropped by around  600 megabytes per second reach speed  when comparing it to last year's 128 gig  SSD so we can clearly see that the SSDs  this year are definitely slower but the  biggest question that we have is will  the slower storage speeds negatively  impact the machines during day-to-day  use and if so by how much and even  though they're slower  are they still fast enough for most  people well for starters we compare the  $1300 base MacBook Pros SSD to Dells  brand new XPS 13 laptop with a 256 gig  SSD and we found that the MacBook was  actually still slightly faster than the  XPS and that's the story for most  Windows laptops packing SSDs that are  slower than the ones that come in Mac  books even with the speeds being slower  this year and interestingly if you grab  the base model and jump to a 256 gig SSD  or larger the write speeds actually go  significantly and that's because the  larger storage drives have more  individual NAND flash chips on them that  on the 128 gig SSD allowing them to work  together to provide faster write speeds  but the point of this testing is that  for many years  Apple was putting in top-of-the-line  SSDs which in my opinion were overkill  for most people especially on the base  models so to find out if those old SSDs  were actually overkill and if these new  ones are still fast enough we decided to  run a few tests to see if the extra SSD  speed actually makes a noticeable  difference in day-to-day tasks so we  took the base 2019 MacBook Pro with a  256 gigabyte SSD upgrade and compared it  to the $1,800 models matching 256 gig  SSD which has around a thousand  megabytes per second faster read speeds  at 2600 megabytes per second instead of  1,600 on the BassPro our first test was  a simple restart test which has to read  and load up all the system files to  start up and the base macbook pro was  only a few seconds behind  we then loaded up two identical  libraries in Final Cut Pro 10 and the  $1,800 MacBook was again just slightly  faster we also opened up Lightroom  classic and saw exactly the same results  as before so it seems like opening apps  and starting up Mac OS is just slightly  faster going from 1600 to 2600 megabytes  per second read speed so in this case I  definitely take higher performance  instead of faster SSD speeds so we moved  on to seeing if we can max out the SSD  speeds during heavy workloads using  Final Cut Pro and Lightroom and then  opening up the Activity Monitor to see  how much data was being read or written  per second and throughout all of our  testing we didn't see it go higher than  87 megabytes per second that's nowhere  near the maximum transfer speeds of even  the slower 2019 SSDs and the main reason  why we only saw a high of 87 megabytes  per second is because the storage has to  wait on the processor and graphics to  render and export the files which is  what you'll experience while using  basically every app out there we then  hooked up one of the most popular  external SSDs Samsung's t5 and  transferred large files to each MacBook  Pro to see which one finished first and  both took a  exactly the same time that's because the  MacBook SSDs can only transfer data as  fast as the connected device can  transfer data and most external SSDs  like the t5 are limited to only around  500 megabytes per second read and write  speeds and basically every flash drive  in every SD card reader even UHS - are  even slower than that  so you basically won't see any  difference in transfer speeds unless  you're using a top-of-the-line  Thunderbolt 3 external SSD which are  extremely expensive and if you're  willing to buy one of those and can  actually put the higher transfer space  to use you most likely will be buying a  higher-end macbook pro with even faster  SSD speeds the point I'm trying to make  is that most people who are in the  market for a budget oriented 13-inch  MacBook probably won't be able to max  out the speed of even the slower SSDs  that come on the new base models and  even though there is a small difference  in boot up speed and loading up apps the  base SSDs are more than fast enough  already  so if I had to choose between having  better processor performance extra  features or a cheaper price instead of  having a faster SSD I would sacrifice  the extra SSD speed every single time so  we've got to give props to Apple for  making this choice because getting $100  off the MacBook Air or getting much more  performance and features on the base  macbook pro for the same exact price is  well worth the slower SSD speeds that  most people in that market probably  won't use anyway and due to the amount  of users that commented on our videos  saying that they're finally going to  pick up a macbook thanks to the awesome  value the new base models pack we'd have  to say that the general public agrees  with us as well if this video helps you  make a decision to buy a new macbook  make sure to use those links down in the  video description below to help out our  channel thanks for watching and make  sure to tap that like button and click  the circle above to subscribe check out  one of those two videos right there and  we'll see you in the next video
 

